msgid "Token Propagation for Quarkus REST"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus-rest-client-oidc-token-propagation` extension provides a REST "
"Client filter, `io.quarkus.oidc.token.propagation.reactive."
"AccessTokenRequestReactiveFilter`, that simplifies the propagation of "
"authentication information."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This filter propagates the xref:security-oidc-bearer-token-authentication."
"adoc[bearer token] present in the currently active request or the token "
"acquired from the xref:security-oidc-code-flow-authentication."
"adoc[authorization code flow mechanism] as the HTTP `Authorization` header's "
"`Bearer` scheme value."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can selectively register `AccessTokenRequestReactiveFilter` by using "
"either the `io.quarkus.oidc.token.propagation.common.AccessToken` annotation "
"or the `org.eclipse.microprofile.rest.client.annotation.RegisterProvider` "
"annotation, for example:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Alternatively, apply `@AccessToken` to individual methods to propagate "
"access tokens for requests made by those methods.\n"
"This lets a single REST client interface call both public endpoints and "
"endpoints that require an access token:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can combine class-level and method-level `@AccessToken` annotations on "
"the same REST client.\n"
"When you do, Quarkus uses the class-level annotation for unannotated methods "
"and the method-level annotation, including its `exchangeTokenClient` "
"attribute, for annotated methods."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Additionally, `AccessTokenRequestReactiveFilter` can support a complex "
"application that needs to exchange tokens before propagating them."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you work with link:https://www.keycloak.org/securing-apps/token-"
"exchange[Keycloak] or another OIDC provider that supports a link:https://"
"tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8693[Token Exchange] token grant, then you can "
"configure `AccessTokenRequestReactiveFilter` to exchange the token like this:"
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Please note that the `exchange-token` configuration property is ignored when "
"the OidcClient name is set with the `io.quarkus.oidc.token.propagation."
"common.AccessToken#exchangeTokenClient` annotation attribute."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you work with providers such as `Azure` that link:https://learn.microsoft."
"com/en-us/azure/active-directory/develop/v2-oauth2-on-behalf-of-"
"flow#example[require using] link:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/"
"rfc7523#section-2.1[JWT bearer token grant] to exchange the current token, "
"then you can configure `AccessTokenRequestReactiveFilter` to exchange the "
"token like this:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`AccessTokenRequestReactiveFilter` uses a default `OidcClient` by default.\n"
"A named `OidcClient` can be selected with a `quarkus.rest-client-oidc-token-"
"propagation.client-name` configuration property or with the `io.quarkus.oidc."
"token.propagation.common.AccessToken#exchangeTokenClient` annotation "
"attribute."
msgstr ""

msgid "Token Propagation for RESTEasy Classic"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus-resteasy-client-oidc-token-propagation` extension provides two "
"Jakarta REST `jakarta.ws.rs.client.ClientRequestFilter` class "
"implementations that simplify the propagation of authentication information.\n"
"`io.quarkus.oidc.token.propagation.AccessTokenRequestFilter` propagates the "
"xref:security-oidc-bearer-token-authentication.adoc[Bearer token] present in "
"the current active request or the token acquired from the xref:security-oidc-"
"code-flow-authentication.adoc[Authorization code flow mechanism], as the "
"HTTP `Authorization` header's `Bearer` scheme value."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `io.quarkus.oidc.token.propagation.JsonWebTokenRequestFilter` provides "
"the same functionality and, in addition, supports JWT tokens."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When you need to propagate the current Authorization Code Flow access token, "
"immediate token propagation works well because the code flow access tokens, "
"as opposed to ID tokens, are meant to be propagated for the current Quarkus "
"endpoint to access the remote services on behalf of the currently "
"authenticated user."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"However, direct end-to-end Bearer token propagation should be avoided.\n"
"For example, `Client -> Service A -> Service B`, where `Service B` receives "
"a token sent by `Client` to `Service A`.\n"
"In such cases, `Service B` cannot distinguish whether the token came from "
"`Service A` or directly from `Client`.\n"
"For `Service B` to verify that the token originated from `Service A`, it "
"should be able to assert new issuer and audience claims."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Additionally, a complex application might need to exchange or update the "
"tokens before propagating them.\n"
"For example, the access context might differ when `Service A` accesses "
"`Service B`.\n"
"In this case, `Service A` might be granted a narrow or completely different "
"set of scopes to access `Service B`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The following sections show how `AccessTokenRequestFilter` and "
"`JsonWebTokenRequestFilter` can help."
msgstr ""

msgid "RestClient AccessTokenRequestFilter"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`AccessTokenRequestFilter` treats all tokens as strings and, as such, can "
"work with both JWT and opaque tokens."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can selectively register `AccessTokenRequestFilter` by using either `io."
"quarkus.oidc.token.propagation.common.AccessToken` or `org.eclipse."
"microprofile.rest.client.annotation.RegisterProvider`, for example:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Alternatively, `AccessTokenRequestFilter` can be registered automatically "
"with all MP Rest or Jakarta REST clients if the `quarkus.resteasy-client-"
"oidc-token-propagation.register-filter` property is set to `true` and the "
"`quarkus.resteasy-client-oidc-token-propagation.json-web-token` property is "
"set to `false`, which is the default value."
msgstr ""

msgid "Exchange token before propagation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the current access token needs to be exchanged before propagation and you "
"work with link:https://www.keycloak.org/securing-apps/token-"
"exchange[Keycloak] or another OpenID Connect provider that supports a link:"
"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc8693[Token Exchange] token grant, you can "
"configure `AccessTokenRequestFilter` like this:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you work with providers such as `Azure` that link:https://learn.microsoft."
"com/en-us/azure/active-directory/develop/v2-oauth2-on-behalf-of-"
"flow#example[require using] link:https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/"
"rfc7523#section-2.1[JWT bearer token grant] to exchange the current token, "
"you can configure `AccessTokenRequestFilter` to exchange the token like this:"
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`AccessTokenRequestFilter` uses a default `OidcClient` by default.\n"
"A named `OidcClient` can be selected with the `quarkus.resteasy-client-oidc-"
"token-propagation.client-name` configuration property."
msgstr ""

msgid "RestClient JsonWebTokenRequestFilter"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Using `JsonWebTokenRequestFilter` is recommended if you work with Bearer JWT "
"tokens whose claims, such as `issuer` and `audience`, can be modified and "
"the updated tokens re-secured, for example, re-signed."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It expects an injected `org.eclipse.microprofile.jwt.JsonWebToken` and "
"therefore will not work with opaque tokens.\n"
"Also, if your OpenID Connect provider supports a Token Exchange protocol, it "
"is recommended to use `AccessTokenRequestFilter` instead, because both JWT "
"and opaque bearer tokens can be securely exchanged with "
"`AccessTokenRequestFilter`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`JsonWebTokenRequestFilter` makes it easy for `Service A` implementations to "
"update the injected `org.eclipse.microprofile.jwt.JsonWebToken` with the new "
"`issuer` and `audience` claim values and secure the updated token again with "
"a new signature.\n"
"The only difficult step is ensuring that `Service A` has a signing key, "
"which should be provisioned from a secure file system or remote secure "
"storage such as Vault."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can selectively register `JsonWebTokenRequestFilter` by using either `io."
"quarkus.oidc.token.propagation.JsonWebToken` or `org.eclipse.microprofile."
"rest.client.annotation.RegisterProvider`, for example:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Alternatively, `JsonWebTokenRequestFilter` can be registered automatically "
"with all MicroProfile REST or Jakarta REST clients if both the `quarkus."
"resteasy-client-oidc-token-propagation.register-filter` and `quarkus."
"resteasy-client-oidc-token-propagation.json-web-token` properties are set to "
"`true`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Update token before propagation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the injected token needs to have its `iss` (issuer) or `aud` (audience) "
"claims updated and secured again with a new signature, then you can "
"configure `JsonWebTokenRequestFilter` like this:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As mentioned, use `AccessTokenRequestFilter` if you work with Keycloak or an "
"OpenID Connect provider that supports a Token Exchange protocol."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Typically, you must prepare two REST test endpoints.\n"
"The first endpoint uses the injected MP REST client with a registered token "
"propagation filter to call the second endpoint."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To learn how it can be done, follow the xref:security-openid-connect-client."
"adoc[OpenID Connect client and token propagation] quickstart, and its xref:"
"security-openid-connect-client.adoc#testing[Testing] section in particular."
msgstr ""

msgid "GraphQL client integration"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus-oidc-client-graphql` extension provides a way to integrate an "
"OIDC client into xref:smallrye-graphql-client.adoc[GraphQL clients], "
"paralleling the approach used with REST clients."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When this extension is active, any GraphQL client configured through "
"properties, rather than programmatically by the builder, uses the OIDC "
"client to acquire an access token, which it then sets as the `Authorization` "
"header value.\n"
"The OIDC client also refreshes expired access tokens."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To configure which OIDC client the GraphQL client should use, select one of "
"the configured OIDC clients with the `quarkus.oidc-client-graphql.client-"
"name` property, for example:"
